+# Research Prompt — Erdős Problem 243
+
+Resolve the following statement.
+
+Let \((a_n)_{n\ge0}\) be a strictly increasing sequence of positive integers.
+Assume
+
+\[
+\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n^2}\to1
+\quad\text{and}\quad
+\sum_{n\ge0}\frac1{a_n}\in\mathbb Q.
+\]
+
+Prove that \(a_{n+1}=a_n^2-a_n+1\) for all sufficiently large \(n\), or give an
+explicit counterexample satisfying every hypothesis and failing that recurrence
+infinitely often.
+
+A complete proof must cover every admissible sequence. A complete disproof must
+give an explicit infinite integer sequence, prove strict increase and the
+square-growth limit, evaluate its reciprocal sum as a rational number, and
+prove infinitely many recurrence failures.
+
+Use the exact rational tails
+
+\[
+T_n=P_n/Q_n,\qquad
+\Delta_n=P_n(a_n-1)-Q_n
+\]
+
+in lowest positive terms as one direct route, but preserve independent
+approaches. The exact identities and route contracts are recorded in
+`APPROACH_REGISTRY.md`.
+
+Return concrete lemmas, equations, divisibility statements, constructions,
+counterexamples to proposed lemmas, or exact failure certificates. Do not count
+as a resolution: a bounded computation, an asymptotic surrogate, a numerical
+experiment, an unchecked solver result, a theorem with a stronger hypothesis,
+or a reformulation that leaves an equally strong unproved assertion.
+
+Audit independently:
+
+- indexing of \(a_n\) and \(T_n\);
+- the estimate for the tail after \(1/a_{n+1}\);
+- reduction by the exact gcd;
+- signs and strict versus non-strict inequalities;
+- positivity and strict increase in any constructed orbit;
+- the implication from an orbit to an actual infinite reciprocal series;
+- the implication from eventual zero tail error to the stated recurrence;
+- consistency with the known Erdős–Straus and Duverney partial results.
+
+Maintain incompatible proof and disproof routes until one is rigorously killed.
+Search aggressively for counterexamples to each proposed intermediate lemma.
+Stop a route if it only reproduces the known denominator/LCM obstruction with
+no theorem-closing invariant. Make no discovery claim before an independent
+referee pass and a fresh literature check.
